```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Router6 is missing a static default route. Its RIP routing table contains
thirteen specific prefixes (RIP-learned and directly connected) but no
0.0.0.0/0 entry in either the FRR RIB or the Linux kernel table. The running
configuration holds only a bare RIP stanza (`router rip / network 10.0.0.0/8`)
with no static route and no `default-information originate` directive.

The mechanism is straightforward: when a packet arrives at router6 destined
for a subnet not explicitly listed in its RIB (any address outside the
RIP-learned set), the FRR/zebra forwarding plane has no matching prefix and
performs a "destination unreachable" drop. RIP-learned destinations continue
to work because those specific prefixes are present; local intra-Location-B
traffic works because the 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 connected routes
are intact. Only traffic to truly external or unknown subnets fails — exactly
the behavior produced by a missing default gateway.

No peer injects a default route via RIP: router3 (10.50.1.1, router6's sole
RIP neighbor on eth2/10.50.1.0/24) carries no `0.0.0.0/0` in its own table
either, ruling out the possibility that RIP default origination was the
original source.

Root cause: the static default route `ip route 0.0.0.0/0 10.50.1.1` (or
equivalent) was removed from router6's live vtysh configuration.

Corrective fix: re-add the static default on router6 via vtysh:
  `ip route 0.0.0.0/0 10.50.1.1`
This installs a default gateway pointing to router3, restoring reachability
to all subnets not explicitly listed in the RIP table.
=== END ===
```